Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe Model (TDMU) v2.342
A Cosmological Framework Based on Discrete Combinatorial Networks
(TDMU V2.342)

Author: mjp

Date: August 3 2026

Abstract
The standard cosmological model (ΛCDM) relies on three exogenous assumptions—the initial singularity, the inflaton field, dark matter particles, and dark energy—whose physical origins remain unexplained within known physics. This paper proposes an alternative framework: the universe is modeled as a topological interface (membrane) emerging from a discrete combinatorial network. The network consists of nodes and directed edges, with local reconnection as its sole dynamics.

Within this framework: space is the totality of distance relations among structures on the membrane; time is the cumulative count of reconnection events; matter consists of stable loop-woven subgraphs (topological knots), whose recursive growth is truncated at the current energy scale; gravity is the statistical effect of the total number of shared links; light is a dynamic transverse wave perturbation propagating through the shared-link network, with speed determined by the intrinsic elastic properties of the membrane medium; nuclear transformations are complementary defect-weaving and forced reconnection (four channels); elemental abundances are determined by a three-factor criterion (synthesis probability, continued aggregation probability, and fission probability); stellar evolution is the dynamic interplay between Path 1 and Path 2 across scales within accretion-dissipation cycles, with stellar types determined by total accretion mass and medium state.

This framework employs no singularity (replaced by a finite critical loop density), no inflation (replaced by the initial conditions of membrane emergence), no dark matter particles (replaced by the shared-link convergence effect of supermassive topological stars), and no dark energy field (replaced by topological tension coupling between the membrane and the external substrate). All observable cosmological phenomena—homogeneous background, large-scale structure, accelerated expansion, elemental abundance distribution—emerge from a single set of geometric rules.

This paper provides a complete axiomatic system, core derivations, a stellar evolutionary spectrum, and testable predictions.

1. Introduction
1.1 Exogenous Assumptions of the Standard Cosmological Model
The contemporary standard cosmological model (ΛCDM) has achieved remarkable observational success, yet its theoretical architecture depends on three exogenous assumptions:

The Initial Singularity: The universe originates from a mathematical singularity where spacetime curvature diverges. At this point, density, temperature, and curvature all tend toward infinity, and general relativity breaks down. A physical theory containing an indescribable singularity at its core indicates that the theory is incomplete.

Inflation: To explain the uniformity and flatness of the universe, the standard model introduces a scalar field that underwent superluminal expansion in the very early universe. This field has no known physical counterpart, and its potential form must be artificially adjusted to match observational data.

Dark Matter and Dark Energy: To explain galactic dynamics and cosmic accelerated expansion, the standard model introduces two unknown components comprising 95% of the total cosmic energy. Dark matter particles have never been detected in terrestrial experiments; the physical nature of dark energy is entirely unknown.

The common feature of these three assumptions: they are not derived from deeper principles but are entities added layer by layer to fit observational data.

1.2 The Path of This Paper
The Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe model (TDMU) starts from a minimalist set of postulates: only nodes, directed edges, shared links, and reconnection counts. From these foundational definitions, all the aforementioned phenomena are derived or reformulated. This paper aims to:

Fully state the axiomatic system (Sections I–II)

Establish the emergence mechanism of material structures (Section III)

Define the four channels of nuclear transformation and the three-factor criterion for elemental abundances (Sections IV–V)

Establish the geometry of shared links and the light-gravity decoupling (Section VI)

Construct the stellar evolutionary spectrum and cosmological framework (Sections VII–VIII)

List testable predictions (Section IX)

Section I: Ontology
Postulate 1: The Substrate

There exists a discrete, background-independent combinatorial network ℳ. It consists of nodes and directed edges connecting nodes.

This network is embedded in no space. It does not evolve in any pre-existing time. The only primitive relation between nodes and edges is "connected" or "not connected." The only primitive property of an edge is its direction—from one node to another.

The sole dynamics of the substrate is local reconnection: when two edges meet at a node, they may disconnect and reconnect according to directional rules. Reconnection is local—it involves only a few edges sharing the same node, and does not involve distant structures.

Direction Rule: On any closed path, when traversing along the path, edges aligned with the traversal direction are counted as positive, and edges opposite to the traversal direction as negative. The signed directional sum over all edges is conserved under local reconnection operations.

The substrate contains no physical quantities. No mass, no energy, no charge, no fields. Only nodes, directed edges, and the connection relations among them.

The substrate is not "nothingness"—it is a definite entity with determinate topological structure and dynamics. Nodes exist, directed edges exist, and local reconnection events occur continuously within it. The substrate is an ever-active topological sea, the raw material of all existence.

Definition 1.1: Loop

A closed path that starts from a node, travels along directed edges, and eventually returns to the starting node is called a loop. Loops are the only structures in the substrate that possess "global recognizability."

Definition 1.2: Topological Charge

On any closed loop, the net signed directional sum along the loop (count of positive edges minus count of negative edges) is an integer. This integer is conserved under reconnection operations—guaranteed by the direction rule.

This integer is called the topological charge of the loop.

Topological charge is not a pre-assumed physical quantity. It is merely a naming of the pure geometric fact of "the signed sum of directions along a closed path."

Postulate 2: Spontaneous Emergence of the Membrane

When the loop density (number of closed paths per unit node count) in some closed region of the substrate exceeds a critical threshold, the loops in that region collectively tend, through reconnection, to weave themselves into a closed surface. This surface divides the region into an "interior" and an "exterior"—with different loop densities inside and outside—and the surface itself becomes the boundary between the two regions.

This closed surface is called a membrane.

The membrane is not "placed into" the substrate. It is the structural pattern toward which the substrate naturally tends under specific loop-density conditions. The membrane is a state of the substrate—the product of a phase transition from the un-woven state to the woven state. The moment of membrane emergence establishes an interior-exterior gradient: the interior is organized into the woven state, while the exterior remains in the un-woven state. Once this gradient is established, the membrane possesses topological persistence and can exist as an independent universe.

Definition 2.1 (Interior-Exterior Distinction of the Membrane): Let membrane M be a connected subgraph of ℳ. Membrane M partitions ℳ into two disjoint sets of nodes—the interior is the set of nodes connected to M but not to infinity; the exterior is the set of nodes connected to M and also to infinity. Membrane emergence is the phase-transition moment at which ℳ first acquires this partitioning property.

Definition 2.2 (Ontological Status of the External Substrate): The external substrate and the internal substrate are ontologically the same entity—both are components of ℳ. Both consist of nodes and directed edges, both obey local reconnection dynamics. The distinction lies only in the organizational mode: the membrane interior is in the woven state, possessing stable loops, a shared-link network, and topological knots; the membrane exterior is in the un-woven state, with loop density below the critical threshold, unable to form stable closed surfaces, and thus lacking a shared-link network and topological knots. The external substrate is not nothingness—it has a definite state, but its dynamical mode does not directly couple to the membrane interior.

Definition 2.3: Topological Gradient Transition Zone (GTZ)

The membrane is not an infinitely thin geometric surface. Structurally, the membrane is a finite-thickness region of continuous transition from the interior woven steady state to the exterior un-woven state, termed the Topological Gradient Transition Zone (GTZ).

Let the radial coordinate r point from the membrane interior toward the exterior, with nominal membrane radius R_mem and transition-zone characteristic thickness σ (σ ≪ R_mem, but σ > 0). The loop density ρ_loop(r) within the GTZ satisfies:

ρ_loop(r) = ρ_crit / (1 + exp((r - R_mem)/σ))

where ρ_crit is the critical loop density required to maintain the woven steady state.

Dynamical Origin of the Sigmoid Curve: Near the critical point of membrane emergence, local reconnection exhibits two competing tendencies—loops in the woven region tend to maintain closed configurations through shared links (positive feedback), while loops in the un-woven region continuously disintegrate due to insufficient topological charge support (negative feedback). These two tendencies interpenetrate spatially, forming a reaction-diffusion-type dynamic equilibrium. The steady-state solution takes the form of a sigmoid curve in the radial coordinate—i.e., the functional form of ρ_loop(r) above. This curve is not arbitrary; it is the simplest steady-state solution allowed by the collective behavior of local reconnection near the critical point: it is the only continuous transition solution that simultaneously satisfies the interior woven steady-state boundary condition (ρ_loop → ρ_max) and the exterior un-woven state boundary condition (ρ_loop → ρ_0) under the competition between "weaving" and "disintegration."

Physical Consequences of the GTZ:

Impedance Mismatch Isolation: The shared-link number density ρ_L(r) decreases monotonically with ρ_loop(r). As light's propagation medium traverses the GTZ, the acoustic impedance Z(r) = ρ_L(r) · c(r) continuously drops to zero. Waves propagating within the membrane encounter perfect impedance mismatch at the GTZ's outer boundary and are totally reflected back into the membrane, unable to transmit through. This is the geometric mechanism for "complete isolation of the universe's interior from the exterior."

Reformulation of Membrane Emergence: Membrane emergence is not the result of the substrate "evolving" through time. Membrane emergence is the bifurcation of the local loop-density distribution function of the substrate from an open configuration to a closed sigmoid configuration. The GTZ is not the membrane's "shell"—it is the membrane's spatial structure itself. The membrane's "thickness" is precisely the finite interval over which the woven state transitions to the un-woven state. Membrane emergence and GTZ formation are two descriptive angles of the same event. At the moment the reconnection count begins within the membrane, the sigmoid curve of ρ_loop(r) already exists as a boundary condition.

Reformulation of Membrane Dissolution: Membrane dissolution (Postulate 12) corresponds to the instability of the GTZ's sigmoid curve—when internal expansion causes the characteristic thickness σ to increase beyond the critical value σ_crit, the gradient layer can no longer maintain a steep transition, the woven state back-percolates into the un-woven state, and the membrane interior is "engulfed" by the substrate. In membrane-internal time, this process manifests as sustained accelerated expansion until the boundary ruptures.

Postulate 3: Hierarchical Weaving

Material structures are composed of topological knots (woven subgraphs that do not spontaneously disintegrate) organized in recursive hierarchies. Interactions between topological knots are mediated through shared links. Each item on this recursive growth tree is a topological knot of some specific order, with its total number of elementary bricks determined by the number of bricks in the preceding generation.

Corollary 3.1: Hierarchical Truncation Criterion

Shared-link networks between topological knots are counted as effective interactions only when the inter-knot distance is less than or equal to a finite integer multiple of the smaller knot's recursive growth radius.

Specifically, let topological knot A be of recursive order m (determined by its elementary brick count N_A, where N_A is some packing configuration of 4^m), with intrinsic combinatorial radius r_A = ⌈N_A^{1/3}⌉ (in node steps).

When the center-to-center distance d_AB > κ·(r_A + r_B) (where κ is a screening factor determined by local loop density, κ ∈ [1, 3]), the total number of shared links N_links decays exponentially below threshold N_thresh, and is effectively zero in macroscopic physics.

Origin of This Truncation: Beyond distances exceeding r_A + r_B, the intermediate loop density in the substrate cannot maintain stable woven channels—any additional links spontaneously disconnect and dissolve due to insufficient topological charge support at intermediate nodes.

Physical Consequences:

At nuclear scales (r ~ 1 fm): The truncation radius greatly exceeds inter-nucleon spacing, and the total number of shared links is geometrically forced to maximum saturation—manifesting macroscopically as the short-range saturating nature of the strong nuclear force.

At atomic scales (r ~ 1 Å): The truncation radius precisely matches the extent of the electron cloud, and the total number of shared links naturally declines with distance according to combinatorial path counts—manifesting macroscopically as the 1/r form of the Coulomb potential.

At cosmic scales (r ≳ 1 Mpc): The truncation radius matches the highest order of the recursive growth tree (galaxy-cluster scales)—beyond this scale, shared-link counts drop to zero.

Section II: Emergence of Spacetime
Postulate 4: Combinatorial Distance

The distance between two nodes is defined by the number of edges along the shortest path connecting them. The distance between two structures is defined by the number of edges along the shortest path connecting their respective regions.

Distance is not a pre-existing metric in space. It is a direct reading of the connection relations among nodes.

Postulate 5: Space

The totality of distance relations among all structures on the membrane constitutes the membrane's spatial structure.

Space is not a container that holds the membrane. It is the collection of relations among structures on the membrane.

When a structure moves, it does not travel through space—it changes its connection relations with all other structures. "Motion" is the record of reconnection history encoded in distance relations.

Postulate 6: Time

The temporal ordering between two events A and B on the membrane is determined by the cumulative number of reconnection events along paths connecting them. If the total number of reconnections along any path from A to B is N, and N > 0, then A precedes B; if N = 0, then A and B are simultaneous.

Time is not a dimension, not a coordinate, not a parameter. It is the cumulative count of reconnection events.

Definition 6.1 (Boundary of Time): Reconnection counting is defined only within the woven steady state (ρ_loop > ρ_crit). The transition of the GTZ's sigmoid curve from an open configuration to a closed configuration is a bifurcation of topological boundary conditions, not a sequence of local reconnections, and therefore consumes no reconnection count. Time begins after the bifurcation is complete—that is, when the first countable local reconnection event appears within the membrane interior. The "instant" of membrane emergence does not exist within time; it is the origin of time itself.

Definition 6.2 (Geometric Criterion for GTZ Internal State): The woven state of loops within the GTZ is metastable—although loop density reaches the critical value, the spatial gradient is too steep for loops to form closed woven subgraphs within finite space. The GTZ itself does not contribute topological knots, but it provides the boundary condition for the existence of all topological knots.

Shared links within the GTZ exist in a "diffuse state": capable of supporting wave propagation (the impedance of light continuously decreases as it traverses the GTZ), but not satisfying the topological-knot stability conditions of Definition 7.1—the number of links is insufficient to form a closed weaving that cannot spontaneously disintegrate. Diffuse-state shared links represent a transitional phase between the woven and un-woven states, serving an impedance-matching role in the boundary conditions of topological knots, but do not themselves constitute material structure.

Section III: Emergence of Material Structures
Postulate 7: Topological Knot

A woven subgraph on the membrane, composed of loops and not spontaneously disintegrating under reconnection dynamics, is called a topological knot.

A subgraph does not spontaneously disintegrate if and only if: the number of consecutive reconnections required to separate it from the rest of the membrane exceeds a threshold. This threshold ensures that no local reconnection operation can release internal stress without destroying the overall structure.

Matter is not pre-existing particles. It is topological knots on the membrane that satisfy stability conditions.

Definition 7.1: Stability Parameters of a Topological Knot

The stability of a topological knot is determined by two factors:

Total number of internal links: The number of independent connection paths between loops within the knot. The larger this number, the more reconnections are required to dismantle it.

Boundary connectivity: The number of connection paths between the knot's surface and the exterior. The lower the boundary connectivity, the harder it is for external reconnections to invade the interior.

Definition 7.2: Topological Defects and Surface Weaving Incompleteness

The surface weaving of any topological knot cannot be absolutely perfectly closed—there always exist regions that cannot be completely covered by the existing weaving, called topological defects. The size and distribution of defects depend on the knot's weaving complexity: knots with fewer elementary bricks weave loosely and have larger defect-area fractions; knots with more elementary bricks weave densely and have smaller defect-area fractions.

Topological defects serve as "active sites" for interactions between topological knots. Whether two topological knots can undergo complementary defect weaving depends entirely on the geometric matching of their surface defects.

Definition 7.2a (Scale Properties of Topological Defects): Topological defects differ only in scale size and distribution patterns; there exists no perfectly defect-free topological knot. Light nuclei weave loosely, with larger defect scales; heavy nuclei weave tightly, with smaller defect scales. Defect scales decrease monotonically with increasing nuclide mass, but never reach zero.

Definition 7.3: Elementary Brick

The smallest topological knot on the membrane that can exist independently and can establish stable shared links with other topological knots is called an elementary brick.

The elementary brick is the effective fundamental unit at the current energy scale. Its "fundamentality" is energy-scale dependent—if future experiments at higher energies reveal that elementary bricks are composed of smaller structures, the model can be naturally extended.

The weaving scale of the elementary brick is determined by the characteristic width of the GTZ's sigmoid curve at membrane emergence. At the instant the sigmoid curve forms, there exists a continuous transition from the un-woven to the woven state within the GTZ. Only loops with scales greater than the GTZ's initial thickness σ₀ can complete closed weaving—smaller loops, unable to span the GTZ gradient, remain in the un-woven state. Therefore, the "minimality" of the elementary brick is not an arbitrary cutoff but is geometrically determined by the boundary conditions of membrane emergence: elementary brick scale ≈ σ₀ (or a fixed multiple thereof). σ₀ is the initial characteristic thickness of the GTZ at membrane emergence; its specific numerical value is determined jointly by the local reconnection microscopic parameters and the critical loop density of the substrate, and serves as a geometric parameter to be determined, constraining it through observations (e.g., CMB spectral features).

Definition 7.4: Inertia of the Elementary Brick

The ability of the elementary brick to resist directional reconnection—i.e., the number of reconnection events required to change its loop's net signed directional sum—is defined as the inertia of the elementary brick. At the phenomenal level, this property is called "mass."

Postulate 8: Recursive Growth and the Topological Knot Spectrum

Topological knots do not exist in isolation. At appropriate energy scales and collision conditions, smaller stable topological knots can combine into larger stable topological knots through complementary defect-weaving mechanisms. When the defects of two smaller knots are geometrically complementary, they interlock to form a larger woven structure—this process is determined entirely by the geometric properties of the defects of both parties.

Smaller knots that can stably combine into larger knots must have defect distributions that satisfy specific compatibility conditions in a topological sense. This naturally generates a recursive growth tree of topological knots—starting from elementary bricks, combining generation by generation, forming stable knots of increasingly complex structure and increasingly large inertia.

Energy-Scale Truncation Rule for Recursive Growth

Recursive growth of topological knots does not proceed indefinitely. At a given loop density (corresponding to the energy scale or temperature at the phenomenal level), only topological knots with inertia below a certain threshold can stably exist. When recursive growth reaches the maximum stable weaving complexity at that energy scale, more complex topological knots cannot form due to insufficient loop-density support, or disintegrate immediately after formation under thermal fluctuations.

At the current low energy scale of the universe (approximately 2.7 K cosmic microwave background), recursive growth is truncated at the baryon-brick level (protons and neutrons). The internal structure of elementary bricks—if it exists—is indeconstructible at the current energy scale, so elementary bricks appear as "elementary particles" at the phenomenal level.

The nature of this truncation is identical to the hierarchical truncation criterion (Corollary 3.1): when weaving complexity exceeds the stability threshold of the shared-link network supportable by the current loop density, higher-order topological knots cannot emerge.

Definition 8.1: Electron Brick

On the recursive growth tree, a stable topological knot composed of 4 elementary bricks in a specific weaving configuration is called an electron brick. It is the smallest independent stable knot at the current cosmic energy scale and the fundamental building block for all more complex material structures.

The internal structure of the electron brick follows the same recursive weaving principle: it possesses a robust core tightly woven from those 4 elementary bricks, and possibly a soft outer layer composed of deeper elementary bricks attached to the core through defects. At the current low cosmic energy scale, the inertial contribution of this outer layer is extremely small and negligible.

Definition 8.2: Exchange Reconnection Number and Geometric Enforcement of Pauli Exclusion

Definition 8.2a (Exchange Reconnection Number 𝒮): Let p_A, p_B be one dangling link port each on the surfaces of two identical topological knots (electron bricks). The minimum number of local reconnection operations required to move p_A to p_B's original position and p_B to p_A's original position is called the exchange reconnection number 𝒮.

Lemma 8.1 (Parity of Exchange): For two identical topological knots occupying the same order on the recursive growth tree, the exchange reconnection number 𝒮 for their ports is necessarily odd.

Proof Sketch: The exchange operation for two identical topological knots must be accomplished through braid exchange (traversing along closed loops, rather than directly dragging nodes through each other). Each local reconnection operation changes the target node of one directed edge. For a complete braid exchange of two identical objects, the length k of the braid group B_N exchange generator sequence must satisfy k ≡ 1 (mod 2). A constructive minimal example requires 3 local reconnections. Therefore, 𝒮 is necessarily odd.

Theorem 8.1 (Geometric Enforcement of Pauli Exclusion): Two identical electron bricks cannot occupy exactly the same geometric position and phase state.

Proof: If two identical electron bricks occupy exactly the same geometric state, the exchange operation degenerates to the identity operation (0 reconnection steps, even). But Lemma 8.1 requires the exchange reconnection number to be odd. The contradiction that an odd number equals an even number forces the assumption to be false. Two identical electron bricks must maintain a difference in at least one geometric degree of freedom—either a phase difference Δθ ≥ 1, or occupation of different spatial winding modes.

Definition 8.3: Baryon Brick and Binary Layered Structure

At orders far above the electron brick on the recursive growth tree, there exist giant stable topological knots woven from large numbers of electron bricks and deeper elementary bricks according to recursive rules.

The structure of such topological knots exhibits a universal binary layered pattern:

Solid Core: Composed of a large number of electron bricks or equivalent elementary bricks in the densest packing configuration. For topological knots reaching macroscopic scales, cubic close packing is the optimal arrangement, and the core brick count approaches integer multiples of 12³ (1728) in the limit. The core provides the high binding energy that resists disintegration.

Soft Outer Layer: Composed of a large number of elementary bricks or small topological knots floating on the core surface, attached to the core through complementary defects, forming a deformable buffer layer. Outer-layer elementary bricks can slide under external forces, absorbing collision energy. The defect distribution of the outer layer determines the macroscopic charge behavior of the topological knot.

These two stable configurations correspond phenomenally to protons (positively charged) and neutrons (electrically neutral). They possess the same structural pattern, differing only in the net topological charge property of the outer-layer defects.

Definition 8.4: Total Compressive Binding Energy (E_comp)

As a closed weaving network, a topological knot possesses a structural threshold against external compression. This threshold is determined jointly by the strength of all connecting edges within the knot, the weaving configuration, and the defect distribution, and is called the total compressive binding energy, denoted E_comp.

E_comp is a single unified physical quantity of the topological knot's resistance to compression, and does not presuppose its internal microscopic decomposition structure. Its essence is the structural resistance of the topological knot, as a closed weaving network, to maintaining its configuration.

Definition 8.4a: Hierarchical Compressive Tensor

The compressive binding energy ℰ_comp of an atomic nucleus decomposes into three geometrically independent contributions:

E_comp = 𝒱_core · 𝒮_core + 𝒱_shell · 𝒮_shell + ℐ(suture)

where:

𝒱_core: Number of elementary brick units in the core close packing, determining the main mass number of the nucleus

𝒮_core: Average link saturation of the core. For perfect close packing (e.g., a 12³ hypercube closure), 𝒮_core → 1

𝒱_shell: Number of effective elementary brick units in the outer shell participating in chemical bonding

𝒮_shell: Saturation of shell ports, determining chemical valence and reactivity

ℐ(suture): Topological stress concentration coefficient at the core-shell transition suture line, determined by the deviation of the suture-layer elementary brick arrangement from the ideal closed configuration

Theorem 8.2: Chemistry-Nuclear Decoupling Theorem

The spatial orientation of shell dangling ports (i.e., chemical valence) depends only on 𝒮_shell and the arrangement pattern of shell ports, and is independent of the total core brick count 𝒱_core. The suture-line stress concentration coefficient ℐ, however, is highly sensitive to discrete changes in the core brick count.

Corollary 8.1 (Vacancy-Induced Stress Concentration): If the core brick arrangement contains vacancies (i.e., the actual brick count is less than the ideal closure count for that weaving-tree order), then the topological stress concentration coefficient at the suture line ℐ > 0. If no vacancies are present, then ℐ ≈ 0.

Corollary 8.2 (Fission Threshold Difference for Uranium Isotopes):

²³⁸U: The core brick arrangement matches the complete closure of a 12³ hypercube, ℐ_238 ≈ 0, fission trigger threshold is high

²³⁵U: The core lacks 3 brick units; the vacancies cause ℐ_235 > 0, fission trigger threshold is significantly lower than that of ²³⁸U

Their chemical properties (𝒮_shell) are identical—the shell port arrangement is unaffected by the core vacancies.

Section IV: Nuclear Transformations—The Four Reaction Channels
4.1 Matter: Stable Topological Knots and Topological Defects
Material particles are stable topological knots on the membrane. Every topological knot carries regions where the weaving cannot be completely closed—topological defects. Defects differ only in scale size and distribution patterns; there exists no perfectly defect-free topological knot. Light nuclei weave loosely, with larger defect scales; heavy nuclei weave tightly, with smaller defect scales.

4.2 General Reaction Scheme and the Four Mechanisms
Interactions between topological knots are essentially the complementary matching of surface defects on both sides and the restructuring of connecting edges. The complete general scheme for nuclear reactions is written as:

a + b → x → c + d

The forward aggregation (a + b → x) is driven by Path 1 or Path 2. After the intermediate nucleus x is formed, its subsequent fate is taken over by one of three pathways: if x's fission threshold is exceeded, it fissions into c and d; if the global topological charges of a and b sum exactly to zero, Path 3 takes over directly, skipping x. The following describes the four mechanisms.

Channel 1: Complementary Defect Weaving (Path 1)

Essential characteristic: defect-induced, stepwise reconnection.

Condition: The topological defects on the surfaces of two topological knots are geometrically complementary.

Process: The defect regions of the two knots interlock, and connecting edges are restructured stepwise in an ordered manner, forming a new stable topological knot x. The energy released depends on the total inertia deficit of the participating topological knots.

Characteristics:

The aggregation probability of Path 1 depends on the geometric matching degree of the surface defects of the two topological knots. As nuclide mass increases, weaving becomes denser and the defect-area fraction decreases monotonically—so the aggregation probability qualitatively decreases with increasing mass. However, this decrease is not absolute; a non-zero aggregation probability exists between any two topological knots.

In environments with sufficient heat dissipation time and thermal expansion space, Path 1 can form a negative-feedback self-stabilizing loop: local temperature rise causes expansion, defect spacing increases, matching probability decreases, and heat release automatically reduces. The gentle burning lasting billions of years in stellar interiors is the operation of this mechanism.

Negative feedback is not automatically obtained but is the product of specific environmental conditions. It requires that the medium possess rapid heat dissipation capability (e.g., fluid convection) and immediate thermal expansion response (density rapidly decreases with rising temperature). Gaseous and high-temperature molten celestial bodies satisfy these two conditions, so internal Path 1 can operate stably over long periods. Solid celestial bodies lack both—but if the internal temperature of a solid body rises to melting, the medium acquires fluid properties and negative feedback can be established.

The aggregation path (stepwise or leapfrog) is dynamically determined by the abundance ratio of raw materials.

Channel 2: Forced Chain Reconnection (Path 2)

Essential characteristic: batch severing, collective restructuring—does not depend on defect matching probability, does not select matching targets; once triggered, proceeds inevitably, and cannot be modulated by environmental feedback.

Condition: The number of local reconnection operations exceeds the batch edge-breaking threshold, i.e., external compression energy exceeds the topological knot's total compressive binding energy E_comp.

Process: Connecting edges are severed in batches, the core weaving network is exposed, and large-scale forced chain reconnection occurs. In a single Path 2 event, a large number of topological knots are simultaneously dismantled and reconnected within an extremely short time, macroscopically manifesting as an instantaneous violent energy release.

Characteristics:

The final state of Path 2 remains topological knots (fragment nuclei c and d).

The trigger condition is universal: the source of external compression energy is not limited to gravity—laser fusion, magnetic confinement, shock waves, any form of extreme pressurization/heating, as long as the injected energy exceeds the nuclide's E_comp, can forcibly trigger Path 2.

Core dismantling in supernova explosions falls into this category.

Channel 3: Topological Annihilation (Path 3)

Condition: The global topological charges of two topological knots a and b sum algebraically to zero.

Process: Complementary defect interlocking does not form a new stable weaving—at the moment of interlocking, the entire topological configuration undergoes complete deconstruction due to the global stress vanishing. All connecting edges are simultaneously removed, and all topological nodes revert to a topologically flat membrane (network ground state). The final state is pure radiation, containing no topological knots. The energy released is equivalent to the total inertia of the participating topological knots, with 100% conversion efficiency.

Characteristics:

In the general reaction scheme a + b → x → c + d, Path 3 corresponds to a + b → pure radiation, completely skipping the intermediate nucleus x.

Path 3 shares the "complementary defect interlocking" trigger condition with Path 1; the difference lies in the outcome of interlocking. The probability of Path 3 depends on the precision of topological charge pairing and is extremely rare under natural conditions.

Path 3 only blocks specific reaction channels (a + b → pure radiation); it does not mean that nuclides of that mass number cannot be synthesized through other precursor combinations.

Channel 4: Stress Tear (Fission)

Condition: After the intermediate nucleus x has been formed via Path 1 or Path 2, the accumulated stress on one of its internal suture lines exceeds the instantaneous tensile limit of the connecting edges. The internal structure of a nuclide is not homogeneous—connecting edges on different suture lines bear different tensile stresses.

Process: The configuration tears mechanically along that suture line, splitting into two smaller nuclei c and d. The energy released equals the decrease in total inertia before and after the tear.

Characteristics:

Fission probability depends on the stress distribution across the internal suture lines of the nuclide. The more concentrated the stress on a given suture line and the closer it approaches the tensile limit of the connecting edges, the higher the fission probability for that nuclide.

Fission probability is one of the three key factors determining whether a nuclide can persist in nature over long timescales (see Section V, Three-Factor Criterion).

4.3 Summary of the Complete Cycle
In the general reaction scheme a + b → x → c + d, the complete cycle is:

a + b → x: Path 1 (defect-induced, stepwise reconnection) or Path 2 (batch severing, collective restructuring);

x → c + d: Fission (stress tear), products returning to the raw material pool;

a + b → pure radiation: Path 3 (topological annihilation), skipping x and directly returning to zero.

4.4 The Deep Mechanism of the Coulomb Barrier
Whether two topological knots can interlock depends on the configurational matching degree of their surface defects. Temperature rise means greater thermal vibration amplitude, exposing defect sites more fully, and increasing the matching probability. The Coulomb barrier, as measured macroscopically, is the statistical failure rate of countless defect-matching attempts. It is not an independent force but the macroscopic statistical manifestation of probability.

Section V: Elemental Abundances and the Iron Peak—The Three-Factor Criterion
5.1 The Three-Factor Criterion
In the reaction network composed of the four channels, the cosmic abundance of any nuclide is determined jointly by the following three probabilities:

Synthesis Probability: The probability that the nuclide is produced through various precursor channels. If a particular channel is blocked by Path 3, that channel's contribution is zero, but the nuclide may still be synthesized through other channels.

Continued Aggregation Probability: The probability that the nuclide, as a reactant, continues to aggregate into heavier nuclides through Path 1 or Path 2.

Fission Probability: The probability that the nuclide fissions back into lighter nuclei due to internal stress tearing.

The three jointly determine the net retention of the nuclide on the mass spectrum:

Net retention ∝ Synthesis Probability × (1 - Continued Aggregation Probability - Fission Probability)

Abundance peaks appear at nuclides where "synthesis probability is reasonably appreciable, but both continued aggregation probability and fission probability are low"—the nucleon flux can reach this point, but once there, it neither easily moves forward nor is easily pushed back, so net accumulation occurs. Abundance valleys appear at nuclides where "synthesis probability is low" or "continued aggregation probability is high" or "fission probability is high"—the nucleon flux either cannot reach them, or cannot stay upon arrival.

The abundance distribution of the entire periodic table is the comprehensive projection of these three probabilities varying continuously across the mass spectrum.

5.2 Leapfrog Paths and Stepwise Paths
In this framework, there is no "mandatory sequential path" on the nuclide mass spectrum. Any two topological knots (regardless of light, medium, or heavy) can aggregate as long as their defects match. The reaction probability depends on the geometric matching degree of the defects of the two parties and has no rigid constraint with their positions on the mass spectrum.

Leapfrog paths and stepwise paths are not mutually exclusive but are two parallel channels whose weights are determined by the dynamic abundance ratio of raw materials. In the early universe, when material abundances were uniform, leapfrog paths became the dominant channel, exponentially accelerating structural growth.

5.3 The Iron Peak: Specific Manifestation of the Three-Factor Criterion
The prominence of iron-56 is the specific manifestation of the above three-factor criterion at mass number 56:

Synthesis Probability: The nucleon flux can reach mass number 56 through both stepwise and leapfrog paths.

Continued Aggregation Probability: The defect-area fraction of the mass-number-56 topological knot is extremely small, so the probability of continuing to aggregate upward as a reactant is low—the nucleon flux "moves forward" slowly here.

Fission Probability: The internal suture-line stresses of the mass-number-56 topological knot are balanced, so the probability of being torn back into lighter nuclei is also low—the nucleon flux is also "pushed back" slowly here.

Superposition: The nucleon flux can reach mass number 56, but both the forward and backward reflux rates are low upon arrival—net accumulation. This is the entire cause of the iron peak. As for which specific isotope at mass number 56 survives, that is determined by that isotope's specific fission probability, and does not affect the fact that the iron peak is a mass-number abundance peak.

The iron peak has no causal relationship with stellar evolution. Iron is prominent in abundance only because the nucleon flux accumulates heavily here. It does not determine any evolutionary turning point of stars. Stars can trigger Path 2 at any stage—unrelated to which mass number happens to form an abundance peak on the mass spectrum.

5.4 The Astrophysical Role of Iron Nuclei
Abundance Level: The position of the iron peak is determined by the three-factor criterion.

Structural Level: The special role of iron nuclei in stellar evolution originates from their extremely high total compressive binding energy E_comp. The Path 1 aggregation probability of iron nuclei is extremely low, but E_comp is extremely high, so the iron core can withstand extremely high gravitational compression energy without triggering Path 2. When the external material continues to accumulate and the gravitational compression energy exceeds the E_comp threshold of the iron core, Path 2 is triggered.

5.5 Examples of Leapfrog Path Reactions
The following reaction equations indicate only mass-number aggregation, without pre-specifying which specific isotope of the product survives. Whether the product is a stable nuclide, unstable isotope, or fissions immediately upon fusion is determined by the fission probability of the topological knot at that mass number.

Two carbon nuclei (A=12+12) → topological knot of mass number 24

Two oxygen nuclei (A=16+16) → topological knot of mass number 32

Carbon + oxygen (A=12+16) → topological knot of mass number 28

Oxygen + neon (A=16+20) → topological knot of mass number 36

Two helium nuclei (A=4+4) → topological knot of mass number 8 (high fission probability, immediately fissions back, or continues capturing helium → topological knot of mass number 12)

5.6 Geometric Necessity of Light Element Enrichment and Its Geological Consequences
The aggregation probability of Path 1 depends on the geometric matching degree of the surface defects of the two topological knots. Light nuclei (carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, etc.) weave loosely, with larger defect-area fractions, so their Path 1 aggregation matching probability is qualitatively higher than that of heavy nuclei. Meanwhile, the particle-number base of light nuclei in the solar system's initial material is far greater than that of heavy nuclei. The superposition of the two—high matching probability and large base—makes the total synthesis amount of light nuclides in the Path 1 aggregation-fission cycle far greater than that of heavy nuclides. This is the geometric origin of the relatively high abundance of life-essential elements such as carbon and oxygen in the universe, requiring no special mechanisms.

Within the Earth's interior, carbon and hydrogen continuously synthesized by Path 1 in the core rise through mantle plumes and deep fluid channels to the crust. The enormous supply of these light nuclides provides the material prerequisite for abiogenic chemical synthesis of hydrocarbons in the crust. TDMU itself does not describe chemical bonding processes, but provides a nuclear-synthesis-level geometric explanation for the deep inorganic origin of hydrocarbons. This is qualitatively compatible with observations of certain ultra-deep oil reservoirs lacking biomarkers and their association with mantle-derived helium (elevated ³He/⁴He ratios).

5.7 Leapfrog Synthesis of Heavy Nuclei in Planetary Interiors (Applied Corollary)
Leapfrog paths allow any two topological knots to aggregate directly without stepwise climbing. Under Earth's outer core conditions (approximately 10⁵⁰ nuclides, pressure 135–360 GPa, temperature 4000–6000 K, integration time 4.5 billion years), two medium-heavy nuclei fusing through leapfrog fusion to generate intermediate nuclei with mass number ≥197, subsequently shedding light fragments to stabilize at mass number 197, can reach appreciable absolute event numbers. The estimated total gold synthesized in Earth's core is approximately 10¹¹ tons—the vast majority remains trapped in the core, with only a minuscule amount exchanging through the core-mantle boundary into the crust (observed crustal reserves approximately 2×10⁹ tons), consistent in order of magnitude.

Section VI: Interactions—The Geometry of Shared Links
Postulate 9: Shared Links

The multiple connection paths between two topological knots, established through intermediate loops on the membrane, are called shared links. Shared links are the sole interaction medium between topological knots.

There exist no independent "force" entities. All interactions are manifestations of the geometric properties of shared links.

Definition 6.0: Equation of State of the Shared-Link Medium

Let the total number of shared links in a local volume V (measured in node steps) on the membrane be N_links. Define:

Shared-link number density: ρ_L = N_links / V

Topological bulk modulus: K_L = -ρ_L · δN_links/δρ_L

where δN_links/δρ_L is the response rate of the total number of shared links to density changes, determined entirely by the local weaving configuration, introducing no external parameters.

Physical meaning of this equation of state: The shared-link network within the membrane possesses intrinsic resistance to compression. Compressing a membrane region (increasing ρ_L) requires additional reconnection steps to maintain weaving stability, and the marginal cost of this input is K_L. Both K_L and ρ_L are purely geometric quantities, derived directly from nodes, directed edges, and reconnection counts.

Definition 9.1: Five Geometric Parameters of Shared Links

All interactions can be reduced to the following five geometric parameters of shared links:

Parameter	Definition	Phenomenal Manifestation
Total link count	Number of independent connection paths between two topological knots	Gravitational strength
Link orientation degree	Statistical uniformity of shared links in spatial directions	Magnetic strength
Link density gradient	Rate of change of link number in space	Electric strength
Link saturation	Ratio of links to all possible connection paths	Strong nuclear force (attraction/repulsion)
Link fragility	Probability of breaking under perturbation	Weak force decay rate
6.1 Geometric Degrees of Freedom Distinguishing Light and Gravity
The shared-link network possesses three independent geometric degrees of freedom:

Degree of Freedom 1: Total link count N_total (static/quasi-static scalar count)

Determines gravity. This total is unaffected by density-gradient potential wells. Even if radial propagating waves are completely confined, the pull-back stress corresponding to the total count still exerts force across enormous radii on external matter. Physical correspondence: gravitational work, rigid torque transmission.

Degree of Freedom 2: Density gradient ∇ρ_link and orientation wave δ𝒜 (dynamic transverse wave perturbation)

Determines light (electromagnetic radiation at the phenomenal level). First/second-order gradient potential wells act only on these transverse waves, not on static tension counts.

Degree of Freedom 3: Density fluctuation longitudinal wave δρ_link

Longitudinal compression-tension stress waves of shared-link density (phonon modes at the phenomenal level). Optical potential wells act only on transverse waves δ𝒜; longitudinal waves are unconstrained by these potential wells.

Core Conclusion (Light-Gravity Decoupling): Gravity (N_total) and light (δ𝒜) are carried by completely independent geometric degrees of freedom. Light being trapped does not imply gravity is shielded; gravity penetrating a trapping surface does not imply light can escape.

6.1.1 Further Elaboration on the Ontology of Light
The ontological status of light in TDMU is radically different from that in the standard model. Light is not an independently existing particle, nor is it a "wave-particle duality" entity. Light is a dynamic transverse wave perturbation (δ𝒜) propagating through the shared-link network, with its propagation speed determined by the intrinsic elastic properties of the membrane medium.

Specifically:

Carrier Medium: Light propagation requires a medium, namely the shared-link network pervading the cosmic membrane. Regions lacking a shared-link network (such as beyond the GTZ outer boundary) contain no light.

Vibrating Content: What vibrates is not electric or magnetic fields, but the spatial orientation arrangement of shared links. When shared links exhibit statistically non-uniform spatial orientation distributions, magnetism arises (static torsional stress fields); when this non-uniformity propagates as transverse waves along the shared-link network, it manifests as light.

Photon: No independent photon particle exists. The "photon" is a local excitation of the discrete phase field Θ(r_i)—a ripple on the shared-link network. Its "particle nature" arises from the discretization constraints imposed by the weaving network on perturbation transmission.

Speed of Light: The speed of light c is not defined by a "maximum reconnection rate," because "rate" implies external time. The speed of light is the instantaneous state parameter of the shared-link network medium itself, determined by the two geometric quantities in Definition 6.0:

c = √(K_L/ρ_L)

This is fully analogous to "the speed of transmission of air pressure in air density at the instant a door closes"—it is an instantaneous reading of the weaving compactness and link density within the membrane, not the cumulative rate of reconnection events. At each moment of uniform membrane expansion, the local configuration of the shared-link network is topologically similar to the previous moment—node number density decreases, but the connection relations between nodes maintain the same topological pattern. K_L describes the resistance of this topological pattern to compression, and ρ_L describes the density of that pattern. As the membrane expands, both scale synchronously by the same geometric proportion, because uniform expansion does not change the topological connection pattern of the local weaving, only its spatial extent. Therefore c = √(K_L/ρ_L) remains constant in uniform regions, possessing universality and isotropy. In the thin layer near the GTZ, ρ_L drops sharply causing local deviations in c, but the impedance mismatch in this layer confines these deviations as evanescent waves within the boundary, preventing them from entering the observable region within the membrane.

Role of Reconnection Counting: Reconnection event counting (time) is the recording scale of "how many nodes the light wave has passed through" as it propagates through the medium, not the driving force determining wave speed. The two are completely decoupled.

6.2 Construction of the Discrete Gauge Field
Definition 9.2 (Combinatorial Covariant Derivative): Let the discrete phase field of shared links be Θ(r_i), defined on lattice node i. Let 𝒜_ij be the orientation tensor contribution of the background shared links between nodes i and j. Define the combinatorial covariant derivative:

D_ij Θ ≡ Θ(r_j) - Θ(r_i) - 𝒜_ij

where:

Θ(r_i) ∈ ℤ is the phase of shared links at node i (reconnection-step count)

𝒜_ij is the background orientation field between nodes i and j due to periodic arrangement of atomic nuclei, defined as the minimum number of reconnection steps along the i → j direction

All quantities are purely discrete, independent of continuous spacetime metrics.

6.3 Geometric Definition of the Superconducting State
Definition 9.3 (Superconducting State): The system is in the superconducting state if and only if the covariant derivative vanishes for all nearest-neighbor node pairs ⟨i, j⟩:

D_ij Θ = 0, ∀⟨i, j⟩ ∈ conducting network

At this point, the system is in global phase locking—all helical motions of shared links are perfectly synchronized.

6.4 Discrete Derivation of London's First Equation
The phenomenal current density J in TDMU is defined as the directed shared-link flux. When the system is in a state deviating from perfect superconductivity but still maintaining phase coherence, the covariant derivative has a residual value. This residual value drives the directed link flux:

J ∝ Σ_{⟨i,j⟩} D_ij Θ

In the continuum limit, D_ij Θ corresponds to the covariant derivative ∇Θ - A. In the London gauge:

J = -(1/Λ) A

where Λ is the geometric inertial constant of the shared-link network, determined by the total number of links per unit volume N_total: Λ ∝ 1/N_total. This is the purely discrete form of London's first equation.

6.5 Geometric Necessity of the Meissner Effect
Taking the discrete curl of the London equation:

∇ × J = -(1/Λ) (∇ × A) = -(1/Λ) B

Inside the superconducting state, all D_ij Θ = 0, so the spatial distribution of J is completely determined by the configuration of 𝒜_ij, and ∇ × J = 0.

It follows that B = 0 strictly holds inside the superconductor. The penetration depth λ_L = √Λ is defined as the geometric yield step count of the shared-link network.

6.6 Topological Necessity of Magnetic Flux Quantization
Consider a closed path C on a superconducting ring. The phase Θ must return to the same discrete count after traversing the ring once, so the total phase change is an integer multiple of 2π.

Summing the covariant derivative along the closed path:

∮_C DΘ · dl = 2π n, n ∈ ℤ

In the superconducting state, DΘ = 0 holds inside the ring body, but along the closed path containing the ring hole, the contribution of A is not canceled. Therefore:

∮_C DΘ · dl = ∮_C A · dl = Φ

Combining yields:

Φ = n · Φ₀, Φ₀ = 2π

Magnetic flux is strictly quantized in integer multiples of the fundamental unit Φ₀ = 2π in TDMU's natural geometric units.

6.7 Geometric Definitions of the Five Fundamental Interactions
Definition 9.4: Gravity

The larger the total number of shared links between two topological knots, the stronger the membrane's tendency to reduce overall stress—this tendency manifests phenomenally as pulling the two knots toward each other. Gravity is the large-number statistical effect of the total count of shared links. No graviton is required.

Definition 9.5: Magnetism

When shared links exhibit statistically non-uniform spatial orientation distributions, a macroscopic torsional stress field arises. The streamlines of this stress field are magnetic field lines. The closed nature of magnetic field lines is a topological necessity—shared links cannot have isolated endpoints in space. Magnetic monopoles are topologically forbidden.

Definition 9.6: Electricity

Density gradients of shared links produce normal stress waves. There exists a stress difference between regions of excess density and regions of deficit density:

Excess-excess (or deficit-deficit) → stress difference is repulsive

Excess-deficit → stress difference is attractive

Electricity is the orthogonal deformation mode of magnetism—the two together constitute the complete linear response of shared-link perturbations.

Definition 9.7: Strong Nuclear Force

When the distance between two topological knots shrinks so that the saturation of shared links approaches maximum:

Shared links form an indivisible shared network → dismantling requires simultaneously severing a large number of links → attraction

If distance shrinks further, shared-link paths are forced to bend → repulsive bow stress arises → repulsion

The strong nuclear force is the nonlinear behavior mode of shared links at extremely short distances.

Definition 9.8: Weak Interaction

When the distance between two topological knots is in the critical range where shared links "can just barely be established," the number of shared links is extremely small. This few-link state is metastable—any local reconnection perturbation can cause link breaking and trigger cascading disintegration. The weak interaction is not a "force" but the spontaneous decay mode of metastable shared-link networks.

Definition 9.9: Phenomenal Electric Current

In conductors, the shared links of topological knots on the atomic nucleus surfaces distribute along closed paths in the absence of external fields—this is the lowest-energy configuration of shared links in cylindrical geometry. External fields (density gradients of shared links) stretch these closed paths into helical paths along the conductor direction. The helically advancing shared links drag peripheral electron bricks to move synchronously. Electric current is the collective manifestation of the helical motion of shared links. Resistance is the statistical frequency of collisions and reconnections of shared links in motion.

Definition 9.10: Superconductivity

When the helical motion of shared links achieves complete synchronization—all links have consistent phase and collision frequency drops to zero—shared links enter a dissipationless transmission state. Superconductivity is the synchronous phase transition of the collective motion mode of shared links.

Section VII: Cosmic Life Cycle and Stellar Evolutionary Spectrum
Postulate 10: Membrane Emergence Event

A region of the substrate reaches a topological bifurcation threshold due to boundary conditions, and the loop-density distribution function ρ_loop(r) transitions from an open configuration to a closed sigmoid configuration (Definition 2.3). Large numbers of loops in opposite directions form paired structures, compressed and contracted by external circulation (whose effect is encoded as the curvature boundary condition at the GTZ's outer boundary, independent of internal membrane time) beyond their elastic recovery point. At the critical point, the system becomes unstable, and topological elastic potential energy drives the loop surface to roll outward, forming a closed membrane.

This event is observed at the phenomenal level as the "beginning" of the universe, but at the foundational level of this framework, there is no time zero—membrane emergence is a phase transition of the substrate from one topological boundary condition to another. Time (reconnection counting) begins accumulating only after the sigmoid curve is formed.

Intuitive Dynamical Picture: An unbounded thick liquid substrate whose local boundary conditions reach a critical phase-transition threshold gives birth to an expanding and ultimately dissipating topological bubble. This is the membrane emergence event of the universe.

Postulate 11: Evolution

After formation, the membrane continues to expand. Expansion causes the overall loop density on the membrane to decrease—at the phenomenal level, this is called "cooling." Temperature is the local manifestation of the loop-density gradient.

During cooling, topological knots emerge successively: elementary bricks → electron bricks → leptons → mesons → baryons → atomic nuclei → atoms → molecules.

Each step corresponds to the most stable weaving structure "permitted to exist" at the current loop density. As loop density continues to decrease, recursive growth is truncated at the baryon-brick level—higher-order topological knots cannot stably exist due to insufficient loop-density support (see Section III, "Energy-Scale Truncation Rule for Recursive Growth").

The membrane's "intermediate state" is determined by the position of the GTZ thickness σ relative to the critical value σ_crit:

σ ≪ σ_crit: Membrane just emerged, weaving active, corresponding to the very early universe

σ < σ_crit and ρ_loop far below the initial emergence value: Weaving already sparse, but GTZ still maintains steep transition—current universe

σ → σ_crit: GTZ begins to degenerate, expansion accelerates, corresponding to the late universe

σ > σ_crit: GTZ destabilizes, membrane dissolves

Observers within the membrane can infer the current value of σ/σ_crit indirectly by measuring the expansion acceleration.

The current membrane is in an intermediate state where "loop density is sufficiently low but not yet low enough for membrane dissolution."

Evolution Direction of σ: The characteristic thickness σ of the GTZ is not a fixed constant but a macroscopic state variable determined by the collective statistics of all reconnection events on the membrane. As the membrane expands, the volume of the internal woven state increases, and the GTZ must cover a larger spherical surface area. Since the loop-density gradient within the GTZ is maintained by the competition between weaving and disintegration, the increase in spherical surface area requires the gradient layer to extend radially—i.e., σ increases monotonically with cosmic expansion.

The rate of increase of σ is determined by the collective behavior of reconnection events within the membrane: the continuous operation of Path 1 injects discrete stress perturbations into the GTZ, tending to blur the gradient layer (σ increases); large-scale synchronized stress releases from Path 2 produce instantaneous compression of the gradient layer (σ temporarily decreases). However, over long time averages, the expansion-driven spherical surface growth dominates the evolution direction of σ. When σ accumulates to the critical value σ_crit, the GTZ's sigmoid curve can no longer maintain a steep transition, and the woven state back-percolates into the un-woven state—this is the trigger condition for membrane dissolution. The specific numerical value of σ_crit is determined by the microscopic weaving-disintegration competition dynamics of the GTZ and serves as a geometric parameter to be determined, constrained by precise observations of cosmic expansion acceleration.

Postulate 12: Final State

The membrane is always part of the substrate. Membrane dissolution is not driven by "external circulation pressure decaying with time," but by the destabilization of the GTZ's sigmoid curve.

When membrane expansion causes the transition-layer characteristic thickness σ to increase beyond the critical value σ_crit, the GTZ can no longer maintain a steep density transition. The woven state begins to back-percolate into the un-woven state—the loop density ρ_loop in the interior region gradually falls back toward the external baseline value ρ_0. When ρ_loop drops below ρ_crit, the weaving network of the entire membrane region loses topological persistence and undergoes a global topological reconnection dissolution—the membrane shatters into discrete loops and returns to the substrate.

In membrane-internal reconnection counting (time), this process manifests as continuously accelerating cosmic expansion, until the boundary layer phenomenologically "ruptures."

The finality of global membrane dissolution refers only to the termination of the membrane as a coherent topological entity, not to the absolute prohibition of complementary defect weaving between local topological knots. Although the Path 1 event rate R of weaving-frozen remnants is always positive, the coherence timescale grows exponentially with topological knot spacing, far exceeding the Hubble timescale.

7.1 Continuous Spectrum of Celestial Bodies
In TDMU, stellar evolution is refined as the dynamic interplay of Path 1, Path 2, fission, and Path 3 at different scales. All celestial bodies follow a single fundamental law at the phenomenal level: the reduction of total inertia is converted into stress-wave energy release.

7.1.1 Solid Planets: Truncation Condition
Solid media have two fatal weaknesses: extremely slow heat dissipation (heat conducted through lattice vibrations, far less efficient than fluid convection), and severely constrained thermal expansion (constrained by the lattice, local temperature rise does not immediately cause density decrease).

Once the Path 1 reaction rate in a solid planet's core enters positive feedback, the solid medium neither dissipates heat fast enough nor expands fast enough to reduce the reaction rate. Local stress rises exponentially, breaking through the batch edge-breaking threshold in an extremely short time. Path 2 is triggered, and the entire planet is dismantled layer by layer from the core outward.

Critical Condition: There exists a critical inertial upper limit for solid planets. For planets exceeding this inertia, regardless of the initial phenomenal temperature, the thermal runaway speed of the core's Path 1 will exceed the lattice thermal conduction speed, triggering irreversible positive feedback until Path 2 detonation.

Survival Condition: If a solid planet's inertia is below the critical value, or if internal radioactive decay/tidal heating is sufficient to heat the core to a molten state, the medium acquires fluid properties—convective heat dissipation and thermal expansion response begin to function. The negative-feedback mechanism of Path 1 is established, and the body may exist stably.

7.1.2 Gas Planets: Smoldering Topological Furnaces
Fluid media dissipate heat rapidly, expand upon heating, and the reaction rate automatically decreases after density drops. From mini-Neptunes to hot Jupiters to brown dwarfs, gas planets stably exist over a wide range of inertias.

Phenomenal Observations: Jupiter's internal heat flow is approximately 5.5×10¹⁷ W; Saturn's internal heat flow is 2.84±0.20 W/m². Path 1 provides a unified explanation: both Jupiter and Saturn cores operate Path 1 complementary defect-weaving reactions, differing only in macroscopic event rate.

7.1.3 Topological Furnace Spectrum
The Sun, Jupiter, and Saturn at the phenomenal level form a complete spectrum from "burning" to "smoldering." Planets are not "failed stars"—they are topological furnaces where gravity has compressed the nuclear fire to just smoldering rather than completely extinguishing it.

7.1.4 Main-Sequence Stars: Core of the Burning Path
When the inertia of a gaseous clump exceeds a certain critical threshold, the core's defect matching probability surpasses a key threshold—the complementary defect matching probability between light-nucleus topological knots rises to a level sufficient to sustain the clump's luminosity.

Essence of Main-Sequence Stars: Dominated by Path 1, under conditions of rapid heat dissipation and immediate thermal expansion feedback provided by the fluid medium, achieving dynamic equilibrium with the environmental thermodynamic response.

Leapfrog Nuclear Burning: The nucleon flux in stellar cores is not a one-way street. Leapfrog paths allow aggregation between any two nuclides. Light-nucleus fusion dominates luminosity, while medium-heavy nucleus fusion proceeds at lower rates in parallel.

7.2 Accretion-Dissipation Cycles and the Complete Stellar Evolutionary Spectrum
The fate of a star is not determined by a unidirectional sequence of "fuel exhaustion → inevitable collapse," but by the dynamic interplay of total accretion mass, accretion rate, envelope dissipation rate, and core vortex decoherence rate. The evolutionary starting point is the gravitational condensation of nebular gas under the shared-link convergence effect. Depending on the total accretion mass and environmental conditions, the condensate differentiates into three initial paths, with migration between paths possible during the star's lifetime due to changes in external mass supply.

Initial Differentiation—Three Paths:

Path A (Smoldering Path): Insufficient accretion mass. After gravitational condensation, the core defect matching probability remains below the stable burning threshold; Path 1 operates only at extremely low event rates; the body does not shine, producing only infrared thermal radiation. This path corresponds to brown dwarfs and gas giant planets (e.g., Jupiter, Saturn). They can exist stably for long periods, with cores smoldering for billions of years.

Path B (Burning Path): Moderate accretion mass. After gravitational condensation and continued accretion, the core loop density surpasses the critical threshold; Path 1 enters negative-feedback equilibrium burning. The star shines stably for billions of years. This path corresponds to main-sequence stars (e.g., the Sun). Main-sequence stars are the natural result of moderate accretion mass, not a special branch. Their stable burning is built upon the rapid convective heat dissipation and immediate thermal expansion feedback provided by the fluid medium (plasma).

Path C (Camouflage Path): Oversaturated accretion. After gravitational condensation and extremely rapid accretion, the central region's loop density far exceeds the critical threshold; a black-hole-class topological star core forms first. External material accumulates around the core as a thick, optically thick envelope. Path 1 dominates in the envelope's surface layer, shining through negative-feedback self-stabilization. Observers see only the envelope's thermal radiation, unable to detect the internal black-hole-class topological star core. This path corresponds to extremely massive stars (e.g., R136a1, ~315 M_⊙) and extremely large-volume stars (e.g., Stephenson 2-18, ~2150 R_⊙). The former is the high-temperature compact-envelope case; the latter is the low-temperature diffuse-envelope case.

The three paths are not strictly isolated. Changes in external mass supply during a star's lifetime can cause migration between paths. A main-sequence star (Path B) can enter the camouflage path (Path C) upon sudden accretion rate increase; a camouflaged star can re-expose as a normal main-sequence star after envelope dissipation, or even degenerate into a smoldering-state body.

Common Subsequent Evolution—Envelope Phase:

Regardless of the initial path, as long as the star possesses an envelope, it enters the envelope evolution phase. The envelope, accumulated from accreted material, is dominated by light nuclei on its surface, with Path 1 complementary defect weaving predominant. Since the envelope is a fluid medium, Path 1 possesses negative-feedback self-stabilization capability: local heat release → expansion → density decrease → defect spacing increase → matching probability decrease → heat release automatically reduces → cooling contraction → density recovery → matching probability recovery.

At this stage, a branch event may be triggered: Local Path 2 Outburst. When the accretion compression rate exceeds the E_comp of local nuclides in the envelope, Path 2 is triggered within the envelope. The gravitational binding of envelope material is relatively shallow; the outburst is usually confined to flares, pulsations, or local mass ejection, without destroying the core structure. Observational correspondences: stellar flares, non-radial pulsations, some novae or dwarf novae outbursts.

When external mass supply declines, the envelope gradually dissipates, Path 1 burning weakens, and luminosity decreases. After the envelope is completely depleted, the core is exposed. The core's properties depend on the initial path and its evolutionary history:

If the core mass is below the black-hole threshold (from low-to-intermediate mass main-sequence stars of Path B), the exposed core is a degenerate thermal remnant—a white dwarf. The white dwarf's core Path 1 is nearly frozen due to insufficient density to sustain high-frequency collisions, shining only through residual heat, slowly cooling. If the white dwarf is in a binary system and accretes material from a companion, its mass grows toward the critical point; Path 1 is reactivated, locally triggering Path 2; due to the lack of negative feedback in the degenerate state, the entire star undergoes instantaneous chain reconnection—Type Ia supernova, complete disintegration, no remnant.

If the core mass is above the black-hole threshold (from Path C, or massive main-sequence stars of Path B evolving until core density surpasses the critical threshold), the exposed core is a superfluid vortex condensate—a black-hole-class topological star (phenomenal "black hole"). This enters the exposed black-hole phase, observationally corresponding to a hungry bare nucleus (e.g., Sgr A*). If external mass is supplied again, the star can re-enter the envelope evolution phase, forming a cycle.

Common Subsequent Evolution—Decoherence Phase:

After black-hole exposure, two fate bifurcations arise.

Mainstream Path—Quiet Decoherence: Vortex twist energy slowly leaks through background longitudinal waves over extremely long timescales; the superfluid state smoothly melts; the black-hole mass gradually decreases. When the mass drops below the critical value required to maintain D_ijΘ=0 global phase locking, the superfluid state collapses, vortices decohere—the frozen vortex twist energy is released, and the weaving network solidifies. The star enters the weaving-frozen state—a solid rigid network, with only extremely low event-rate Path 1 operation. In this phase, lacking fluid negative feedback, the solid medium's accumulated stress periodically triggers local Path 2, producing pulsed radiation. Observational correspondences: pulsars, neutron stars. If Path 2 triggers on a larger scale, it manifests as magnetars. Further accretion causing mass growth can return the star to the black-hole state.

Rare Branch—Unstable Explosion (Core-Collapse Supernova): During decoherence, local fluctuations in the vortex network trigger chain Path 2, and the total energy release exceeds the residual gravitational binding energy. This is the product of three independent probabilities—local fluctuation probability × chain propagation probability × binding-overcoming probability—far less than 1. When conditions are satisfied, the entire vortex core disintegrates, and the frozen vortex twist energy is released concentratedly in an extremely short time. Observational correspondences: Type II, Ib/c core-collapse supernovae. Ejected material returns to the nebula, closing the cycle. The vast majority of black holes transition quietly through decoherence to the weaving-frozen state; supernovae are the rare complications of decoherence.

Final Destination—Weaving-Frozen Remnants:

The weaving-frozen state continues cooling; Path 2 permanently ceases; only unobservable Path 1 at extremely low event rates remains. The body is completely non-luminous, becoming a cooled solid nucleus.

Complete Cycle Closure:

At any stage of the evolutionary tree, changes in external mass supply can cause path migration. Weaving-frozen-state bodies in binary systems can accrete companion material, causing mass growth and re-entering active phases. Supernova-ejected material returns to the nebula, initiating a new round of gravitational condensation. There is no true "final state," only dynamic switching between Path 1 and Path 2 at different stages and scales. Stars are transient states in the accretion-dissipation cycle.

7.3 Compact Topological Stars (Pulsars): Path 1 Accumulation → Periodic Path 2 Triggering
When the energy release of Path 2 is insufficient to completely disrupt the body, the remnant is compressed to the extreme by gravity. In this extreme compression state, the topological knot sphere is compressed to its limit; the surface structure collapses—the defect fraction approaches 100%, and the entire body is in a "fully surface-reconnectable" state.

Definition of Compact Topological Star: A compact aggregate of topological knots compressed to the state where the defect fraction approaches 100%, with no distinction among "stable nuclides." In the compressed limit state, the surface weaving of topological knots has been completely destroyed, and all nodes are in a connection-saturated state—every edge in the network has reached its maximum coordination number and cannot accept new connections. This is connection-saturated rigidity—the topological counterpart of phenomenal "degeneracy pressure."

Pulse Radiation Mechanism—Stress Accumulation-Trigger-Release Cycle:

Phase 1: Path 1 operates continuously in the solid network. A minuscule proportion of nuclides independently reach reaction conditions locally, releasing tiny amounts of heat and stress. With the enormous total number of particles, the cumulative effect of these events causes the stress in the central local region of the star to continuously approach the E_comp threshold.

Phase 2: When the accumulated stress in the core region reaches E_comp, Path 2 is triggered—connecting edges are severed in batches, and chain reconnection initiates. Only a small fraction of nuclides in the core high-energy region need to reach criticality. Path 2 releases a shock wave within milliseconds. The propagation speed of the shock wave in the solid network is determined by the medium's sound speed, estimated to be on the order of 0.3c to c. With neutron star radius ~10 km, the shock wave traverses the full star in approximately 0.03–0.1 ms.

Phase 3: The shock wave propagates through the solid network toward the entire spherical surface. The wavefront is extremely narrow, with extremely short duration passing through any given position. When the shock wave reaches the surface layer, its compression exceeds the surface layer's instantaneous tolerance limit, producing a "high-explosive squash-head" spallation effect, ejecting charged fragments. Observers at extreme distances receive signals only from extremely narrow line-of-sight directions.

Phase 4: After pulse release, the star returns to Path 1, re-accumulating stress, forming the cycle.

Large-Number Law Proof of Period Stability: Millisecond pulsars (P ~1 ms) require timing precision with period relative fluctuations not exceeding 10⁻¹⁵. With the total particle number of the star ~10⁵⁷, the number of Path 1 events per single period far exceeds 10³⁰; Poisson statistical relative fluctuations are far below the femtosecond level. Femtosecond-scale period stability is the inevitable consequence of the law of large numbers.

Glitch (Sudden Period Change): Corresponds to abnormally concentrated stress accumulation in a specific core region, triggering a larger-scale Path 2, local structural reorganization, and sudden changes in the stress accumulation rate or trigger threshold. Magnetars: Correspond to larger-scale Path 2 triggering, with higher shock-wave energy radiating into the X-ray/gamma-ray band. Death Line: No sharp boundary—continuous mass loss progressively lengthens the accumulation time, shock-wave intensity weakens, intermittency increases, and eventually Path 2 no longer triggers; pulses cease. After cessation, Path 1 continues at extremely low rates, with weak thermal radiation.

7.4 Topological Stars (Phenomenal "Black Holes")
Aggregates of topological knots whose weaving activity is nearly frozen. Path 2 is completely quiescent—internal stress remains below the E_comp threshold of all nuclides, so forced chain reconnection no longer triggers. Path 1 mathematically never reaches zero, but its event rate can be suppressed far below the observable window of the current cosmic age, appearing macroscopically as "quiescent."

The core of a topological star is a globally phase-locked superfluid vortex condensate (D_ijΘ = 0). Energy released by Path 1 and Path 2 is locked into vortex twist energy through a three-step mechanism: first, reconnection events produce local stress shock waves (thermal phonon intermediate state); second, outward escape is trapped by the radial shared-link gradient potential well, while inward dissipation is blocked by the absence of dangling ports due to connection saturation; third, phonon accumulation to the nonlinear threshold is transformed into quantized topological vortices through collective reconnection of the shared-link network. All released energy is frozen in the form of vortex twist energy, producing no normal light radiation.

Topological stars do not rely on singularities or event horizons. They are the exposed cores after the dissipation of camouflaged-star envelopes—a phase in the accretion-dissipation cycle, not the endpoint of evolution.

7.5 Quasars: Imprisoned Internal Circulation Reconnection Storms
Quasars are the most luminous objects in the universe, with typical luminosities reaching 10⁴⁰–10⁴¹ W and variability timescales as short as days. In the very early universe, primordial topological knot clumps have their core Path 2 chain-reconnection waves blocked by the convergence effect of the surrounding enormous shared links, unable to penetrate the clump boundary, forming a sustained internal circulation reconnection storm.

Quasars do not pass through phenomenal main-sequence stellar progenitor phases. In the complete TDMU life-cycle picture, quasars correspond to the "full-load active" phase—their cores are already formed compact topological stars, with envelopes as thick accretion layers supplied at high accretion rates.

7.6 Shared-Link Origin of Planetary Magnetic Fields
Planetary magnetic field generation does not require specific material compositions but is the geometric result of the shared-link network achieving directional alignment at planetary scales. Path 1 in planetary cores continuously releases heat, driving convective motion in liquid regions. Planetary rotation, as a global inertial mold, constrains random thermal convection into large-scale toroidal vortices symmetric about the planetary axis—this macroscopic ordered fluid is equivalent to large-scale directional alignment of shared links. According to Definition 9.5, this directional alignment closes head-to-tail at the core scale, emerging as a stable dipole magnetic field.

Magnetic polarity corresponds to the chirality (left-handed or right-handed helicity) of the shared-link network. Geomagnetic reversals are global chirality phase transitions of the outer-core circulation crossing topological bifurcation points—when Path 1 heat outputs from the northern and southern hemispheres show asymmetric accumulation, the asymmetry is amplified under rotation, forcing the global chirality of the shared-link network to flip. The irregularity of reversal intervals originates from local Path 2 events randomly injecting reverse seed fiber bundles into the circulation. The slow drift of magnetic poles is the meridional precession of the fluid symmetry axis.

Unification Test of This Mechanism: Venus has a mass similar to Earth but extremely slow rotation (243 days); the lack of rotational constraint causes fluid motion to be merely local random turbulence, and shared links cannot close on large scales, so there is no intrinsic dipole magnetic field. Jupiter's metallic hydrogen produces a magnetic field, proving that magnetism does not depend on iron-nickel composition—the essence of magnetic fields is the directional alignment of shared links, not specific materials.

Section VIII: Geometric Interpretation of Cosmological Observations
8.1 Cosmic Microwave Background Radiation
The cosmic microwave background radiation is the topological snapshot left at the critical transition moment when the membrane interface went from violent change to equilibrium—the instant when weaving structures were frozen.

Homogeneous Background: The geometric mean state of the initial conditions at membrane emergence. No inflation is required to explain homogeneity; homogeneity is the starting point of membrane emergence.

Temperature Fluctuations (Anisotropy): Statistical fluctuations of local shared-link density on the interface, locked in at the freezing moment. Hot spots correspond to slightly higher density; cold spots to slightly lower density.

Multipole Structure of the Angular Power Spectrum: Spatial projection statistics of topological structures at different scales on the interface at the freezing moment.

8.2 Large-Scale Structure
Initial homogeneity within the membrane is broken by statistical fluctuations of local reconnection. Fluctuations are amplified by the positive feedback of Path 1. Leapfrog paths allow cross-order aggregation, accelerating structure formation.

No dark matter halos are required as gravitational seeds.

8.3 Galactic Rotation Curves
The gravitational effects of galactic dark matter halos originate from the shared-link convergence effect of supermassive topological stars (black-hole class, masses > 10⁵ M_⊙). These objects are extremely few in number and extremely massive; their microlensing timescales are extremely long (on the order of years), and current survey data cannot exclude them as the primary component of dark matter halos.

Key Distinction:

Low-mass compact objects (<30 M_⊙) correspond to solid weaving-frozen states and do not constitute the main body of dark matter halos

Supermassive compact objects (>10⁵ M_⊙) correspond to fluid vortex states and constitute the main body of dark matter halos

A natural mass gap exists between the two classes, determined by topological phase-transition conditions.

Response Regarding Dynamical Friction: Supermassive topological stars generate long-range topological stress fields through shared-link convergence effects. When two topological stars approach too closely, shared links reach connection saturation, producing repulsive bow stress that prevents them from sinking to the galactic center. This causes supermassive topological stars to be "frozen" at various positions in the halo during the early stages of galactic evolution, forming an extended mass distribution.

8.4 Cosmic Accelerated Expansion
The topological tension coupling between the membrane and the external substrate continuously injects energy as the membrane expands, driving accelerated expansion.

At the phenomenal level, this continuous energy injection manifests as accelerated cosmic expansion—the observational effect attributed to dark energy in the standard model. No new fields or new entities are required.

8.5 Elemental Abundance Distribution
The cyclic dynamics of leapfrog paths (aggregation-fission cycles), integrated over time, produce the observed elemental abundance distribution. Every particle is an intermediate state of time and probability.

8.6 High-Redshift Massive Structures
Leapfrog paths allow direct aggregation to form massive topological stars in the early membrane. No seed black holes plus long-timescale accretion are required. This provides a natural explanation for massive galaxies observed by JWST at high redshifts (z>10).

8.7 Topological Coupling Mechanism of the Multi-Membrane Universe
The ontological status of the substrate (Postulate 1) implies that membranes are not the only possible structures. If the loop density in some region of the substrate exceeds the critical threshold, membranes can emerge spontaneously. This means multiple membranes—multiple universes—may exist in the substrate, each emerging independently, each with its own internal woven state and shared-link network.

8.7.1 Fundamental Constraints on Inter-Membrane Interactions
The substrate region between two membranes is in the un-woven state. The un-woven substrate lacks a shared-link network, so light (δ𝒜) cannot propagate between membranes. There is no "light communication" between membranes in the usual sense.

However, this does not mean membranes are completely isolated. The un-woven substrate still consists of nodes and directed edges, still obeys local reconnection dynamics. Any violent dynamical process in one membrane will generate local reconnection perturbations in the substrate, propagating as reconnection-event waves through the substrate and possibly reaching another membrane.

8.7.2 Three Channels of Inter-Membrane Interaction
Channel 1: Direct Impact of Substrate Reconnection Shock Waves

Any violent dynamical process in another external membrane—its internal Path 2 events (supernova explosions or core collapse), or overall oscillations of the membrane itself—will generate local reconnection perturbations in the substrate. These perturbations are not light but reconnection-event waves of the substrate.

When the reconnection-event wave reaches the surface of our cosmic membrane, it interacts locally with the surface topological defects (Definition 7.2). Since the membrane surface is the interface between the woven and un-woven states, surface topological defects are the only sites that can interact with external perturbations. Observable manifestations of this interaction include:

Collective excitation of membrane surface defects, manifesting as coherence anomalies in the cosmic microwave background radiation that cannot be explained by internal fluctuations

Energy injection in specific directions, possibly triggering local weaving reconstruction on the membrane surface, manifesting phenomenally as diffuse gamma-ray bursts or X-ray flashes that cannot be attributed to known sources

It must be noted that reconnection-event waves propagating through the un-woven substrate rapidly dissipate due to the lack of a coherent medium, with intensity decaying exponentially with distance. This means that only external membranes sufficiently close to ours can produce observable impact events.

Channel 2: Indirect Effects of Topological Tension Coupling

There exists topological tension coupling between the membrane and the external substrate (extension of Postulate 5). If another massive external membrane exists, its own topological tension will alter the substrate stress field in the region of our membrane.

Anisotropy in the substrate stress field would cause anisotropic accelerated expansion of our universe—small systematic differences in the Hubble constant in different directions. This difference would not be random but should exhibit large-scale dipole or quadrupole patterns correlated with the direction of the external membrane.

Current cosmological observations have already revealed the so-called "Hubble tension"—significant discrepancies in the Hubble constant measured by different methods. TDMU attributes this to anisotropic stresses from external topological structures, rather than measurement systematics or new physics. This explanation yields a clear observational prediction: the directional dependence of the Hubble constant should exhibit systematic patterns, not random distributions.

Channel 3: Topological Defect Handshake of Membranes—An Extremely Rare Large-Scale Coupling

If the distance between two membranes shrinks so that their surface topological defects can directly contact, complementary defect weaving between the surfaces of the two membranes may occur.

Complementary defect weaving is the fundamental interaction mechanism between topological knots in TDMU (Path 1). The same mechanism can operate between surface defects of two membranes. When the surface defects of two membranes happen to be geometrically complementary, they interlock to form a shared-link "bridge" connecting the two membranes.

The establishment of this bridge is an instantaneous phase-transition event. Afterward, a shared-link pathway appears between the two membranes for the first time. Light (δ𝒜) can propagate from one membrane to the other along this bridge. Such an event might manifest phenomenally as:

An ultra-high-energy radiation burst appearing suddenly in a region of the universe, inexplicable by any known astrophysical process

Accompanying the burst, transient, localized, measurable deviations in the fine-structure constant or the speed of light—because the shared-link networks of the two membranes undergo temporary overlap, altering the local shared-link network properties

If the bridge persists stably, it becomes a permanent channel connecting the two membranes. At the phenomenal level, this corresponds to the concept of a wormhole in standard cosmology, but with a completely different ontological foundation: the wormhole is not a topological connection of spacetime, but a shared-link bridge between two woven membranes.

8.7.3 The Multi-Membrane Universe and the Ultimate Constraint on Civilization Survival
TDMU's multi-membrane coupling mechanism provides a physical formulation for an ancient philosophical question: Can a civilization survive after its parent membrane dissolves?

From Postulate 12, any membrane—whether naturally emerged or artificially constructed—will eventually dissolve. Membrane dissolution means the collapse of all weaving structures within the membrane, including all topological knots, all shared-link networks, and all matter and life based on these structures. There are no exceptions.

Therefore, for a civilization to surpass the lifetime limit of a single membrane, it must master inter-membrane migration technology. This includes two possible paths:

Path 1: Construct an artificial membrane (Ark). Before the parent membrane dissolves, the civilization can use the matter and energy within the parent membrane to construct a sub-membrane that independently maintains weaving stability. The sub-membrane survives the shock wave of the parent membrane's dissolution and then navigates through the substrate, seeking another natural membrane and establishing a shared-link bridge with it. The technical challenges of this path have been discussed in Section VII.

Path 2: Find an existing external membrane and establish connection in advance. If the civilization can detect a neighboring external membrane and establish a shared-link bridge with it before the parent membrane dissolves, it can transfer its population and information to the external membrane at the moment of dissolution. This path avoids the engineering challenges of constructing a sub-membrane but requires extremely stringent conditions: the external membrane must be within sufficiently close distance, its weaving pattern must be compatible with the parent membrane, and its surface defects must be capable of complementary weaving with the parent membrane's surface defects.

Regardless of which path is chosen, the civilization faces a fundamental limitation: the substrate lacks a shared-link network, so light cannot propagate between membranes. During inter-membrane migration, the civilization will be in complete information blindness—they cannot perceive the external environment through light, cannot know in advance the internal state of the destination membrane, and cannot communicate with any external entity during the voyage. They can only perceive the world through the tremors of the substrate itself.

This is the deepest constraint TDMU imposes on civilization: membranes are oases of existence; the substrate is an endless desert. The journey across the desert is blind, solitary, and irreversible. The ultimate fate of civilization depends not on how far it develops within a single membrane, but on whether it can learn to migrate continuously across the generations of membranes—building the next ark before each membrane dissolves, finding the next home.

There is no endpoint. Only cycles. Meaning is not conserved; only reconnection endures.

Section IX: Testable Predictions
The following lists the testable predictions of the TDMU framework. Each is stated in "if...then..." form to ensure clear falsifiability.

Prediction 9.1: Compact Object Mass Gap

Prediction: There should be a scarcity of stable compact objects in the phenomenal ~2.5–5 M_⊙ interval. Any objects found in this interval should exhibit transitional states. If a large number of stable compact objects are found in this interval, the framework requires revision.

Prediction 9.2: Solid Planet Inertial Upper Limit

Prediction: Purely solid planets exceeding a certain critical inertia cannot exist stably. If a large number of long-term stable purely solid planets are found at arbitrary inertial intervals, this framework is falsified.

Prediction 9.3: Extreme Star Asteroseismology Test

Prediction: The asteroseismic inertia M_seismo of extreme stars should be significantly greater than the luminosity inertia M_lum, with ratio M_seismo/M_lum > 1.5, monotonically increasing with radius. If the ratio is approximately 1, the hidden-core hypothesis is invalid.

Prediction 9.4: Magnetic Flux Quantization Structure

Prediction: Magnetic flux must be an integer multiple of some irreducible fundamental unit. If magnetic flux is observed in any superconducting system not to be an integer multiple of some irreducible fundamental unit, this framework is falsified.

Prediction 9.5: Impact Signatures of External Membranes

Prediction: If the cosmic microwave background radiation contains large-scale coherent structures that cannot be explained by internal fluctuations and are concentrated in specific sky regions, these may be topological defect excitation events from external membranes. If such structures are absent in higher-precision CMB surveys, this channel is inactive within the current observational window.

Prediction 9.6: Anisotropic Expansion

Prediction: If the Hubble constant exhibits systematic differences beyond statistical errors in different sky regions, and the difference distribution shows large-scale dipole or quadrupole patterns, this may indicate topological tension coupling from external cosmic membranes. If the directional dependence of the Hubble constant disappears at higher precision, this prediction is falsified.

Prediction 9.7: Transient Signals of Inter-Membrane Bridging

Prediction: If an ultra-high-energy radiation burst is observed in the universe that cannot be explained by any known astrophysical process, and this burst is accompanied by transient, localized, measurable deviations in the fine-structure constant or the speed of light, this may correspond to a topological defect handshake event between two membranes. If all such bursts are not accompanied by transient deviations in these physical constants, this channel is not activated within the observational window.

Section X: Retrospect
The entire content of physics—space, time, matter, gravity, magnetism, electricity, nuclear force, weak force, electric current, superconductivity, nuclear transformations, elemental abundances, stellar evolution, the topological phase-transition origin and final state of membranes, the geometric enforcement of quantum statistics, the geometric decoupling of isotope fission, the topological origin of superconducting gauge fields—all reduces to three things:

Nodes, directed edges, and shared links.

Plus two emergence principles:

Recursive weaving and defect complementarity.

And two truncation rules:

The hierarchical truncation criterion (the geometric window for effective interactions determined by recursive growth radii) and the energy-scale truncation rule (recursive growth truncated at the highest weaving complexity that can stably exist at the current loop density).

Theorem 8.1 re-anchors Pauli exclusion—one of the most central statistical rules in quantum field theory—from a mysterious consequence of the "spin-statistics theorem" onto the geometric enforcement of braid exchange. Two identical topological knots cannot occupy exactly the same geometric state, not because they obey Fermi-Dirac statistics, but because exchanging their ports requires an odd number of reconnection steps, while the identity operation requires zero steps (even)—the contradiction that an odd number equals an even number forbids complete overlap of states at the purely combinatorial level.

There are no absolute "elementary particles," only eternal recursion and emergence—and where recursion is truncated by the energy scale, there "elementary particles" appear. There is no true "final state," only dynamic switching between Path 1 and Path 2 in the accretion-dissipation cycle. Stars are not unidirectional arrows pointing to endpoints, but transient states in cyclic networks—brown dwarfs, main-sequence stars, extreme stars, white dwarfs, black holes, pulsars, supernovae—all are different projections of the same set of geometric mechanisms onto the three coordinates of total accretion mass, medium state, and evolutionary stage.

In Section VIII, we go further: our membrane—our universe—may not be the only self-organizing vortex in the substrate. There may be other membranes, each with its own internal spacetime and physics, each experiencing emergence and dissolution. There is no light communication between membranes—light is merely ripples on the shared-link network within a membrane and cannot traverse the un-woven substrate. Yet they are not completely isolated from each other. The tremors of the substrate itself transmit the presence of each to the other. If a membrane forms or dies sufficiently close by, our universe would feel a silent ripple across the topological sea—not seen in light, but heard in the rhythm of expansion.

On a summer night, at the bottom of a pond, a toad lies still in the water, blowing bubbles. The bubbles rise, expand, deform, oscillate, and finally burst, returning to the ripples of the water.

In this image:

The water is the substrate—that invisible, all-bearing foundation. There may be other bubbles in the water, each independently rising, expanding, and bursting. There is no direct sound transmission between bubbles, but they are all in the same water, and the tremors of each are transmitted through the water.

The bubble wall is the cosmic membrane—the ordered structure emerging from the substrate. The bubble wall is not an infinitely thin soap film, but a continuous transition layer from the water (substrate) to the bubble interior (woven state)—the density of the water continuously decreases from outside to inside at the bubble wall. This is precisely the GTZ's sigmoid curve.

The rising of the bubble is time—the irreversible unfolding.

The expansion of the bubble is the cooling and evolution of the universe.

The texture of the bubble is matter—the woven structures on the membrane, their complexity truncated by the current energy scale.

The bursting of the bubble is the final state—structural dissolution, return to the substrate.

The toad still lies quietly in the unreachable depths of the substrate. We are born on the ripples of the membrane and can never look back at it throughout our lives—but that does not prevent us from gradually reconstructing its appearance through the topological imprints on the membrane.

Why do I retain this image? The toad blowing bubbles is the starting point of my model. After many rounds of iterative deepening, I cannot abandon it. Without this image, none of this would exist. So I still call it the Toad Bubble Universe Model. And I prefer to call my topological stars Toad Stars.

Meaning is not conserved; only reconnection endures.

